 2. 👋 Tesla&#x27;s farewell to cars 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: David Paul Morris/Bloomberg via Getty Images Elon Musk's spectacular unveiling of a flying, rocket-powered Tesla Roadster could be seen as a boisterous farewell to Tesla, the car company, Axios' auto expert Joann Muller writes. 

 Why it matters: The Roadster is a dazzling celebration of automotive engineering, but Musk increasingly envisions a future in which humans don't drive at all — and Tesla is more an AI and robotics company than an automaker. 🖼️ The big picture: With speculation mounting about a possible Tesla-SpaceX merger, the Roadster offers a tantalizing glimpse of how the two companies' technologies might intersect.

 Tesla's first Roadster, introduced nearly 20 years ago, helped launch the electric vehicle revolution by proving that EVs could be fast, sexy and fun. The new Roadster takes that idea to an almost absurd extreme, using SpaceX rocket technology and powerful suction fans to perform gravity-defying stunts. And while Tesla can still push the boundaries of automotive engineering, its mainstream vehicle lineup is aging, sales have struggled and Musk is increasingly focused on robotaxis, Optimus robots and artificial intelligence. The intrigue: Thursday's demo arguably had little to do with selling cars.

 Not every version of the Roadster will be street legal, and Musk had some fun while acknowledging the risks of driving a rocket-powered car. "This is not necessarily a safety-optimized car," he said Thursday. "But ... it takes the fun meter to 11 out of 10." 🏁 The bottom line: The original Roadster announced Tesla's arrival as a car company. The new one might be its most extravagant exit.

