 3. 🌹 DSA&#x27;s biggest test 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images Black voters may determine whether Democratic Socialists of America (DSA) can build on breakthroughs in the 2026 elections, Axios' Russell Contreras and Andrew Pantazi write. 

 Why it matters: This year's primaries showed that DSA's path runs through older Black voters who wield enormous influence in Democratic primaries — particularly in the South — but have been slower to embrace insurgent candidates. 🖼️ The big picture: Older Black voters have long been the Democratic Party's kingmakers.

 Many prize electability, imposing what New America's Theodore Johnson calls a "viability hurdle" before taking a chance on someone outside the establishment. Zoom in: This year's primaries offered fresh tests of that dynamic — particularly among young Black voters.

 🐊 In Florida, state Rep. Angie Nixon became the first DSA member to win a major-party statewide nomination. Her vote share rose in precincts with younger and larger Black populations. 🎸 In Detroit, Donavan McKinney's victory in a primary for Michigan's 13th congressional district showed a DSA-backed candidate can break through in a center of Black political power. ⚾ But in St. Louis, progressive former Rep. Cori Bush lost to more moderate Rep. Wesley Bell. She led among younger voters, but just 19% of the Black likely-primary electorate was under 50. Share this story . 

 
 
