 4. 🎙️ Exclusive: Musk tells Isaacson &quot;AI and robots&quot; need to fix deficit 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Walter Isaacson talks to Elon Musk in 2023 at SpaceX's launch and development facility in Starbase, Texas. Photo: Courtesy of Simon &amp; Schuster Elon Musk tells biographer Walter Isaacson in a new podcast interview that the fizzled DOGE experiment, which the SpaceX and Tesla CEO once headed, proved there's "no way we were gonna solve the deficit from within the government."

 "The only way we're going to solve the national debt is with AI and robots," Musk tells the author for a new podcast series, " The Builders with Walter Isaacson ," premiering Tuesday. "We're not gonna solve it by trying to save our way there." Why it matters: Musk's comments to Isaacson — author of the definitive 2023 biography, " Elon Musk " — admit that DOGE was a flop, but point to the chance that some future administration can take a new stab at the problem using ever-more-capable AI.

 🔬 Zoom in: Musk said the appetite to cut spending "is not there at the presidency, the House, the Senate or the judiciary."

 "Maybe if the president made that his primary goal, we could do that," Musk added. "If you try to solve the deficit and stop wasteful spending, you make a lot of enemies. President Trump likes to be liked. He doesn't like people calling him, being upset about ... some funding being stopped." Musk admits he "kinda got carried away, basically" and that he wound up being "gone for too long" from his companies.

 "What I should have done," he added, "was be more in the background ... and probably only had it last for a month or two" rather than the four months he was formally in the White House. "I should have gone back and focused on AI a little sooner than I did." The big picture: Isaacson warned this week that Steve Jobs and the other great creators of the personal computer age would be cautious about autonomous AI agents not tightly connected to human control.

 Isaacson includes his warning in the new preface to an updated edition, published this week, of his sweeping 2014 history of the digital age, " The Innovators : How a Group of Hackers, Geniuses, and Geeks Created the Digital Revolution." Behind the scenes: Isaacson tells me he launched "The Builders" because he wanted to press the builders of this new AI age on these dangers and hear in their own voices what motivates them.

